12. { -KEY- } cxd3 $1 { This improves White's structure since: a) It controls the c4- and e4-squares (the main point) b) White can use the open c-file c) Black can't use his queenside pawns to open files (not relevant in this game) d) The pawn can advance to d4 e) It closes the a6-f1-diagonal. 
'It may seem strange to attach an exclamation mark to a move which today would be automatically made by any candidate master' wrote Kasparov in the book series on his predecessors. 'But then, in 1885, the consequences of the pawn capture were by no means obvious, and the majority of players were simply unable to think long-term.' } ( { According to Kasparov, Black had to play } 12. cxd3 b4 { followed by ...a6-a5 and ...Ba6. } ) 
 ( 12. cxd3 a5 { has the same idea. } ) 
 ( 12. cxd3 d4 { opens the long diagonal for the light-squared bishop, but also gives up the e4-square. } 13. Nf2 $14 ) 

