12. { -KEY- } f4 $1 { The way to increase the pressure. Black doesn't want to capture on either e4 or f4 since it would help White's knights forward. However, the exf5 and especially fxe5 exchanges tend to be good moves for White. Another important factor is that Black's knight on a6 is misplaced. In this Czech Benoni set-up, it would have wanted to chase away White's bishop with ...Nb8-d7-f6-g8. Remember this manoeuvre, as there will be no further reminders in this book. I initially wanted to include an exercise with the knight manoeuvre, but it was eventually discarded since Black's position was too bad. By the way, White could have made some other move and followed up with f2-f4, but the sooner the better. } Nc7 ( 12... Bf6 { defends e5, but the bishop will be less effective than a knight on the central square, because it will be vulnerable to knight threats. There are many good continuations, } 13. Rae1 Nc7 14. fxe5 Bxe5 15. Nf4 { being one example. } ) 
13. exf5 ( 13. Rae1 { was the game, where Black eventually escaped with a draw. } ) 
gxf5 ( 13... Bxf5 14. fxe5 { gives Black an isolated pawn. White continues with h2-h3 and g3-g4, then puts a knight on e4. } ) 
14. g4 $1 { This is not forced, but it's both strong and instructive. } fxg4 15. fxe5 dxe5 16. Rxf8+ Bxf8 17. Ng3 $18 { White's pieces will smoothly join the attack. } 
